"EFPA Test Review Model - Version 2025 - EFPA (https://www.efpa.eu/resource/efpa-test-review-model-version-2025/)\nciteturn6search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.2 years ago; Crawled: 2 days ago; # EFPA Test Review Model – Version 2025\n\n  * 25 August 2025\n\n# EFPA Test Review Model – Version 2025\n\nThe Model for the Review, Description and Evaluation of Psychological and Educational Tests (Test Review Model) developed by the European Federation of Psychologists’ Associations AISBL (EFPA) provides a structure for descriptions and rigorous evaluations of psychological assessments, tests, scales, profiles, and questionnaires used in work and organisational, educational, clinical and health (including clinical neuropsychology), sports, forensic, counselling, coaching and other contexts. It is designed to be adapted to reflect different national arrangements for psychometric testing and to be applied by expert reviewers.\n\nReview information presented in this structure will support developers, authors, suppliers, publishers, and trainers to improve tests and testing practice. It will also inform policy makers in defining and supporting standards and, in particular, in creating a test review programme. In turn this will help users make the right assessment choices by providing authoritative, unbiased and consistent reviews of tests.\n\nFull document here attached:\n\nefpa_test_review_model_v2025_5-1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMODEL FOR THE REVIEW, DESCRIPTION AND (https://www.efpa.eu/sites/default/files/2025-08/efpa_test_review_model_v2025_5.pdf)\nciteturn6search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; © 2025 European Federation of Psychologists’ Associations AISBL (EFPA). ... Evaluation of Psychological and Educational Tests (Test Review Model): https://www.efpa.eu/resources\nThis version (5.0) was published in August 2025 by the European Federation of Psychologists’ Associations \nAISBL (EFPA) \nRue Marché aux Herbes 105/39, B- 1000 Bruxelles, Belgique. \nhttps://www.efpa.eu/  \n \n© 2025 European Federation of Psychologists’ Associations AISBL (EFPA). This work is openly licensed \nvia CC BY-SA 4.0. \n \nTo view a copy of this licence, see CC BY-SA 4.0 Deed | Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International | \nCreative Commons. \n \nThe images or other third party material included in this paper are included in the paper’s Creative \nCommons licence, unless indicated otherwise in a credit line to the material. If material is not \nincluded in the paper’s Creative Commons licence and your intended use is not permitted by statutory \nregulation or exceeds the permitted use, you will need to obtain permission directly from the \ncopyright holder. \n \nEvery effort has been made to trace copyright holders and to obtain their permission for the use of \ncopyright material. EFPA apologises for any errors or omissions and would be grateful to be notified \nof any corrections that should be incorporated in future reprints or editions of this paper. \n \nRecommended citation:  \nEuropean Federation of Psychologists’ Associations AISBL (EFPA, 2025). Model for the Review, Description and \nEvaluation of Psychological and Educational Tests (Test Review Model): https://www.efpa.eu/resources--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMore Testing News (https://www.intestcom.org/upload/media-library/ti-52jun25-1748840884G1hzN.pdf)\nciteturn6search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.4 years ago; Update of the EFPA Test Review Model: ... TRM publication is anticipated for July alongside the BoA Symposium and Round Table at ECP 2025, Pathos.\nMore Testing News\n\nUpdate of the EFPA Test Review Model:\nnow in its final stage before publication\n\nThe European Federation of Psychologists (EFPA) Board of Assessment (BoA) has updated its Test Review Model (TRM). This model has been used extensively as a standard across Europe in evaluating tests (with many translations around the world).\n\nAlong with the utilisation of technology in assessment, new topics have arisen since the inception of the previous 2013 version, so a revision was very necessary to remain up to date.\n\nThe BoA Working Group tasked itself to start and finish the project within the 2023-25 EFPA mandate. Interim progress along with the wider impact of BoA work was communicated at the ITC Conference 2024 in Granada through a pan European double Symposium.\n\nThe final TRM draft was completed December 2024, where it was then put out officially for consultation to the 37 Country Member Associations. Major test stakeholders including test publishers, assessment providers, and academic institutions were invited to comment.\n\nThrough an extended workshop held at the University of Ghent Test Lab, and an ongoing follow through, all comments have now been addressed and appropriate revisions made.\n\nA finalising process through EFPA HQ and Board of Directors is taking place in June. TRM publication is anticipated for July alongside the BoA Symposium and Round Table at ECP 2025, Pathos.\n\nEFPA BoA would like to thank all those ITC members who took part in the consultation for their valuable contributions.\n\nNigel Evans, Chair and Convenor,\nBoard of Assessment | EFPA\n\nThe EFPA TRM work group: Helen Baron, Ana Hernandez, Ursula Brzezinska, Nigel Evans (BoA Convenor), Ian Florance, Dragos Iliescu, Mark Schittekatte (TRM Chair)\n\n6--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLietuvos psichologų kongresas (https://www.psichologusajunga.lt/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/LPK2026_santrauku_leidinys_ISBN9789955159438.pdf)\nciteturn6search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Insights from the EFPA flagships: \nTest Review Model and the EuroTest – ... summer of 2025.\nInsights from the EFPA flagships: \nTest Review Model and the EuroTest – \na test user qualification framework\nĮŽVALGOS IŠ PAGRINDINIŲ EFPA INICIATYVŲ:  \nTESTŲ VERTINIMO MODELIS IR „EUROTEST“ –  \nTESTŲ NAUDOTOJŲ KVALIFIKACIJOS SISTEMA\nUrszula Brzezińska1, Ian Florance2, Mark Schittekatte3\n1Pracownia Testow Psychologicznych Polskiego Towarzystwa Psychologicznego, Poland; \n2European Test Publishers Group, UK; 3Ghent University, Belgium\nThe aim of the EFPA Board of Assessment (BoA) was to develop and impro-\nve assessment practice in Europe. It was one of the oldest and largest \nworking groups of the European Federation of Psychological \nAssociations (EFPA). Tests and test use are changing under the \nimpact of technology and other issues. We will share insights \nand impact(s) of the work of the BoA, in particular on using \nthe updated European Test Review Model, published in the \nsummer of 2025. The panel will also address broader initia-\ntives aimed at improving the quality of test use, including the \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDevelopment of the psychopathological vulnerability index for screening at-risk youths: a Rasch model approach | npj Mental Health Research (https://www.nature.com/articles/s44184-024-00106-6)\nciteturn6search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.9 years ago; Crawled: last month; The steps for the construction of the test were developed according to the Standards for Educational and Psychological Testing, which delineates four phases of the test development process: test specifications, item development and review, assembling and evaluating test forms, and developing procedures and materials for administration and scoring^{21}. ... Integrating these frameworks was essential because the standards for educational and psychological testing provide international benchmarks for test and item construction, while Downing’s guidelines offer best practices grounded in years of experience and research literature from various areas of psychometrics and educational measurement.\n### Development steps for PVI\n\nThe steps for the construction of the test were developed according to the Standards for Educational and Psychological Testing, which delineates four phases of the test development process: test specifications, item development and review, assembling and evaluating test forms, and developing procedures and materials for administration and scoring^{21}. Additionally, the steps inspired by the test development guidelines proposed by Downing (2006), which encompass an overall plan, content definition, test specifications, item development, test design and assembly, test production, test administration, scoring responses, determining passing scores, reporting results, item banking, and producing a technical report. The test construction process also reflected the author’s experience in instrument development. Integrating these frameworks was essential because the standards for educational and psychological testing provide international benchmarks for test and item construction, while Downing’s guidelines offer best practices grounded in years of experience and research literature from various areas of psychometrics and educational measurement. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPracownia Testów Psychologicznych PTP (https://practest.com.pl/aktualnosci/2026-07-31-icap)\nciteturn6search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Jednym z głównych tematów dyskusji był zaktualizowany EFPA Test Review Model 5.0, opublikowany w 2025 roku.\n\n# Pracownia Testów Psychologicznych PTP na ICAP 2026 we Florencji\n\nImage\n\n#### Minuta czytania • piątek, 31 lipca 2026\n\n \n\n### Pracownia Testów Psychologicznych PTP na ICAP 2026 we Florencji\n\nPracownia Testów Psychologicznych Polskiego Towarzystwa Psychologicznego była reprezentowana podczas Międzynarodowego Kongresu Psychologii Stosowanej (ICAP 2026), który odbył się we Florencji.\n\nUrszula Brzezińska wzięła udział jako panelistka w międzynarodowej sesji „Insights and Impacts for Applied Psychologists Using Tests, in Particular with the Updated EFPA Test Review Model”, poświęconej doświadczeniom oraz kierunkom rozwoju standardów stosowania testów psychologicznych w Europie.\n\nPanel zgromadził ekspertów zaangażowanych w działalność Board of Assessment Europejskiej Federacji Towarzystw Psychologicznych (EFPA). Jednym z głównych tematów dyskusji był zaktualizowany EFPA Test Review Model 5.0, opublikowany w 2025 roku. Prace nad nową wersją Modelu trwały trzy lata i koncentrowały się nie tylko na jego aktualizacji, ale również na dostosowaniu do współczesnych wyzwań stojących przed diagnozą psychologiczną.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nXI Conference –European (https://congress.eam2025.eu/event/2/book-of-abstracts.pdf)\nciteturn6search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Thursday, 24 July 2025 ... Board of Assessment (BoA) of the European Federation of Psychologists’ Associations (EFPA) ... focuses on tests: the Test Review Model.\nThursday, 24 July 2025\nBook of Abstracts - XI Conference –European Congress of Methodology\nSymposium : ”Standards and Guidelines for Educational and Psychological Assessment: Continuing the Conversation”\n2.4\nSymposium : ”Standards and Guidelines\nfor Educational and Psychological Assessment:\nContinuing the Conversation”\nTitle\nDifficult Conversations in Revising the Standards for Educational and Psychological Testing\nAuthor(s)\nStephen Sireci 1\n1 University of Massachusetts Amherst\nAbstract\nPsychological tests are essential tools that help psychologists make decisions about people. The\nBoard of Assessment (BoA) of the European Federation of Psychologists’ Associations (EFPA)\nhas various projects aimed at improving tests and testing practices across Europe and beyond.\nIn this presentation, we share two BoA projects. The first is the BoA’s flagship project, which\nfocuses on tests: the Test Review Model. This model provides a systematic framework for\nreviewing and assessing psychological tests based on several criteria (materials, reliability, va-\nlidity, norms, etc.). It has recently been updated to incorporate aspects of digital assessments,\ninclusivity, and diversity, among others. The second project focuses on test user standards,\ndefining the competencies and skills required to ensure proper test use. In this presentation,\nwe will highlight the main features of these projects, emphasizing their impact and the chal-\nlenges of implementation.\nPage 189--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMetric Quality of Intelligence Tests in Spain: Evidence from 13 National Test Commission Reviews - PMC (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13608864/)\nciteturn6search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: 6 days ago; Second, they demonstrate that systematically coded institutional test-review reports constitute a viable, underused data source for the empirical study of psychometric practice, a methodological approach that could be extended to other national review systems built on the EFPA model. ... A recent study found high agreement between a verbal-comprehension test generated by a large language model and the WAIS-III Verbal Comprehension Index, although with a small sample and difficulties in automated scoring (Hadar-Shoval et al., 2025).\nThe EFPA model has been progressively updated to incorporate recent developments in measurement (Hernández et al., 2022). Its underlying philosophy is especially relevant for intelligence tests, because it does not merely assess isolated psychometric indicators. It proposes a comprehensive review that includes the quality of materials, theoretical foundation, adaptation, validity, reliability, norms, and the level of detail of the available documentation (Evers et al., 2013). The quality of a test is thus no longer understood as a single property. It comes to be understood as the result of several sources of evidence and different criteria of appropriate use (AERA et al., 2014).\n\nIn Spain, Prieto and Muñiz (2000) adapted these early European developments and proposed their own model for evaluating test quality. This model was embodied in the Test Evaluation Questionnaire (Cuestionario para la Evaluación de los Tests [CET]), designed to combine descriptive information, quantitative rating, and qualitative judgment (Ponsoda & Hontangas, 2013; Prieto & Muñiz, 2000). ### 2.4. Instruments\n\nThe central instrument of this study is the Test Review Questionnaire-Revised (Cuestionario de Evaluación de Tests Revisado; CET-R). This questionnaire is based on the test evaluation model of the European Federation of Psychologists’ Associations (EFPA) and on international measurement standards (Evers et al., 2013; Hernández et al., 2016). The CNT has applied it systematically since the fifth edition of the project, replacing the original CET developed by Prieto and Muñiz (2000).\n\nThe CET-R is organized into three sections. The first provides a general description of the test. The second assesses the test’s psychometric characteristics. The third provides an overall evaluation in the form of a conclusion. Quantitative responses use a 1-to-5 scale with the following ratings: 1 (Inadequate), 2 (Adequate but with shortcomings), 3 (Adequate), 4 (Good), and 5 (Excellent). Some individual items also include an additional not-applicable (N/A) category, which covers both information that was not reported in the manual and information that does not apply given the characteristics and purpose of the specific test being evaluated.\nIssues such as the traceability of training data or the absence of conventional manuals echo challenges previously identified in the evaluation of the quality of non-commercial tests (Abad, 2024; Guilera & Barrios, 2025).\n\nThese three developments share a common challenge. Test-review models, including the CET-R, were designed with fixed-form instruments and stable norms in mind (Evers et al., 2013; Prieto & Muñiz, 2000). Adaptive formats, algorithmically generated items, and tests based on generative artificial intelligence require rethinking which evidence should take priority. Organizations such as EFPA are already working in this direction (Hernández, 2026). However, not even intelligence tests, the domain with the strongest psychometric tradition, have yet resolved classic shortcomings such as DIF or IRT-based reliability. The immediate priority, therefore, is not only to monitor new technologies. It is to first consolidate the quality standards that already exist.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe science behind JTI — reliability, validity and independent review | JTI (https://onlinejti.com/en/about/science)\nciteturn6search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; The EFPA Test Review Model was developed by the European Federation of Psychologists' Associations to evaluate psychological tests on their theoretical basis, documentation, materials, norms, reliability and validity. ... The 2025 reliability study used the online version of JTI.\n\nIndependent review\n\n## Certified by DNV against the EFPA Test Review Model\n\nThe EFPA Test Review Model was developed by the European Federation of Psychologists' Associations to evaluate psychological tests on their theoretical basis, documentation, materials, norms, reliability and validity. DNV certifies tests against this model; the assessment is carried out by two independent reviewers, who examine the documentation and the research behind it.\n\n### Current certificate\n\nImage: DNV\n\nCertificate\n    DNV-767718-2025-NOR\n\nIssued\n    19 May 2025, by DNV Business Assurance Norway\n\nValid until\n    28 September 2028\n\nFirst certified\n    28 September 2017\n\nAssessed against\n    EFPA Test Review Model 4.2.6 and NS-EN ISO/IEC 17065:2012\n\nScope\n    JTI – Jungian Type Index, Norwegian version, for work and occupational purposes\n\nDNV's 2025 assessment\n    Approved for personal development purposes\n\n### Review history\n\n  1. 2017\n\nFirst certification\n\nFull review against the EFPA model of the JTI documentation, materials, norms, reliability and validity. Certified for development use, not for selection.\n\n  2. 2021\n\nRecertification\n\nDocumentation review; certificate renewed for three years. For the next review, the reviewers asked for a longer-interval retest study and criterion-related evidence.\n\n  3. 2024–25\n\nNew studies\n\nAt DNV's request, a 240-person retest study with an average interval of 9.5 months and an occupation study of over 40,000 respondents.\n\n  4. 2025\n\nRecertification\n\nApproved for personal development purposes. The current certificate is valid until 28 September 2028.\n\n### What the certificate covers\n\nThe certificate covers the Norwegian version of JTI, which is the questionnaire every JTI uses: the other language versions are translations of it, with the same 56 items. In our 2026 analysis, internal consistency in each language version is .75 to .85. The 2025 reliability study used the online version of JTI.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPublicada la versión actualizada de 2025 del Modelo de Revisión de Test de la EFPA - Infocop (https://www.infocop.es/publicada-la-version-actualizada-de-2025-del-modelo-de-revision-de-test-de-la-efpa/?hilite=tranquilizantes)\nciteturn6search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Crawled: last month; Publicada la versión actualizada de 2025 del Modelo de Revisión de Test de la EFPA ... Model For The Review, Description And Evaluation Of Psychological And Educational Tests (Test Review Model)\n\nPublicada la versión actualizada de 2025 del Modelo de Revisión de Test de la EFPA\n\n15 Sep 2025\n\nLa Federación Europea de Asociaciones de Psicólogos (EFPA, European Federation of Psychologists Association, AISBL) ha publicado la nueva versión actualizada de su modelo para la revisión, descripción y evaluación de test psicológicos y educativos. Este modelo de revisión de test proporciona una estructura para descripciones y evaluaciones rigurosas de pruebas, escalas, perfiles y cuestionarios psicológicos, utilizados en el ámbito del trabajo, la educación, la clínica y la salud (incluida la neuropsicología clínica), los deportes, la medicina forense, la orientación, etc., y está diseñado para adaptarse a las diferentes normativas nacionales sobre pruebas psicométricas y para su aplicación por parte de revisores expertos.\n\nEsta nueva versión ha sido elaborada por un grupo de trabajo de la Junta de Evaluación de la EFPA, compuesto por expertos y expertas en el ámbito de la evaluación psicológica, entre ellos, Ana María Hernández Baeza, coordinadora de la Comisión de Test del Consejo General de la Psicología de España (COP).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEvaluating instruments for assessing teaching practices in teacher preparation programmes: insights from a systematic review | Educational Assessment, Evaluation and Accountability | Springer Nature Link (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11092-026-09514-y)\nciteturn6search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 weeks ago; Crawled: yesterday; Standards articulate a benchmark or “bar” that frames expectations of practice (CCSSO, 2013; Darling-Hammond, 2017), codifying representations of teachers’ work (Wyatt-Smith & Looney, 2016), and offering public assurance of quality (Danielson, 2024). ... These critiques echo UNESCO’s Reimagining Our Futures Together report (2021), which called for rethinking teacher education in line with broader system priorities and strengthening international comparability in evaluation and qualifications (UNESCO, 2021).\n### 1.1 Evaluating teaching competence: challenges and standards\n\nA persistent challenge in teacher education lies in how competence is conceptualised and operationalised in evaluation instruments. Raths and Lyman (2003) warned that the lack of professional agreement on what constitutes competence has allowed candidates to progress into the profession despite notable weaknesses, while Donaldson (2010) argued that judgements of excellence based on professional standards often approximate randomness. Prior research has revealed limitations in summative observation forms, which often failed to distinguish meaningfully among levels of teaching effectiveness (Arends, 2006). Without clear criteria, evaluators’ judgements may diverge from performance assessments, particularly at the extremes of performance (Sandholtz & Shea, 2011).\n\nProfessional teaching standards have been introduced as one response to this challenge to define. Standards articulate a benchmark or “bar” that frames expectations of practice (CCSSO, 2013; Darling-Hammond, 2017), codifying representations of teachers’ work (Wyatt-Smith & Looney, 2016), and offering public assurance of quality (Danielson, 2024). --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFriday, 04 July 2025 (https://ecp2025.eu/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/HANDBOOK-ECP-2025-30-6-1.pdf)\nciteturn6search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.3 years ago; Friday, 04 July 2025 ... <tr><td>10:00 - 11:00</td><td><b>Symposium 35</b> - The EFPA Test Review Model – a revised tool for associations, providers, and users of Tests &amp; Assessments<br>Chair: Nigel Evans<br><br>10:00 The Revision of the EFPA Board of Assessment (BoA) Test Review Model: delivering a necessary and thorough update<br><i>Mark Schittekatte</i><br>10:15 Updates to the EFPA Test Review Model: A Focus on Reliability and Validity<br><i>Ana Hernández</i><br>10:30 Advancing Test Evaluation Practices: A Comprehensive Review Process Guided by the EFPA Test Review Model<br><i>Urszula Brzezińska</i><br>10:45 Strategic issues and stakeholder positioning for the EFPA Test Review Model<br><i>Ian Florance</i></td></tr>\nFriday, 04 July 2025  \n(program per room)\n\nAkamas C\n\n<table id=\"e1\">\n<tr><td>09:00 - 10:00</td><td><b>Roundtable discussion 21</b> - Evolutions on the IPCP: New work done<br>Chair: Alex Siegel<br><br>09:00 Evolutions on the IPCP: New work done<br><i>Alex Siegel</i>, Rachel Phillips, <i>Nicola Gale</i>, Gladys Mwiti, Christine Roland-Levy</td></tr>\n<tr><td>10:00 - 11:00</td><td><b>Symposium 35</b> - The EFPA Test Review Model – a revised tool for associations, providers, and users of Tests &amp; Assessments<br>Chair: Nigel Evans<br><br>10:00 The Revision of the EFPA Board of Assessment (BoA) Test Review Model: delivering a necessary and thorough update<br><i>Mark Schittekatte</i><br>10:15 Updates to the EFPA Test Review Model: A Focus on Reliability and Validity<br><i>Ana Hernández</i><br>10:30 Advancing Test Evaluation Practices: A Comprehensive Review Process Guided by the EFPA Test Review Model<br><i>Urszula Brzezińska</i><br>10:45 Strategic issues and stakeholder positioning for the EFPA Test Review Model<br><i>Ian Florance</i></td></tr>\n<tr><td>11:00 - 11:15</td><td>Coffee Break</td></tr>\n<tr><td>11:15 - 12:00</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>12:00 - 13:00</td><td><b>Symposium 39</b> - Digital Empowerment of Psychologist to Address Increasing Mental Health Needs Across Europe<br>Chair: David Gosar<br>Discussant: Maria Karekla<br><br>12:00 Digital interventions in systematically differentiating psychological care: Current insights and implications for practice<br><i>David Dias Neto</i><br>12:20 Interdisciplinary training as a key factor in e-mental health<br><i>Lise Haddouk</i><br>12:40 The Digital Navigator: GeCng digital mental health in routine care right<br><i>Tom Van Daele</i></td></tr>\n<tr><td>13:00 - 14:00</td><td>Lunch</td></tr>\n</table>--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNieuw EFPA Test Review Model gelanceerd | Thomas More (https://assessmenthub.be/nl/nieuw-efpa-test-review-model-gelanceerd)\nciteturn6search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.2 years ago; Crawled: last month; # Nieuw EFPA Test Review Model gelanceerd.\n\n27/08/2025\n\nBlog\n\n# Nieuw EFPA Test Review Model gelanceerd.\n\n27/08/2025\n\nSteven Joris Onderzoeker\n\nVandaag werd het vernieuwde EFPA Test Review Model officieel gepubliceerd. Dit model vormt de Europese standaard voor het beoordelen van psychologische tests en biedt psychologen, onderzoekers en testontwikkelaars een duidelijk en actueel kader om de kwaliteit van psychodiagnostisch materiaal te evalueren.\n\nHet EFPA Test Review Model heeft als doel de transparantie en betrouwbaarheid van psychodiagnostiek te versterken. Door middel van duidelijke criteria helpt het professionals om beter onderbouwde keuzes te maken bij het selecteren en inzetten van tests in de praktijk. Daarmee ondersteunt het model de verdere verankering van evidence-based praktijkvoering binnen de geestelijke gezondheidszorg.\n\nEen belangrijk voordeel van het model is dat het ook de internationale vergelijkbaarheid van testbeoordelingen vergroot. Hierdoor kunnen professionals in heel Europa zich baseren op dezelfde kwaliteitsstandaarden, wat samenwerking en kennisuitwisseling bevordert.\n\nVanuit Vlaanderen mocht Steven Joris vanuit Thomas More als expert in psychodiagnostiek bijdragen aan de herziening van dit model. Het resultaat is een evenwichtig en toekomstgericht kader dat aansluit bij de hedendaagse uitdagingen van psychologisch testen.\n\nHet vernieuwde EFPA Test Review Model is vanaf vandaag beschikbaar en kan geraadpleegd worden via de website van EFPA.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCharting the Future of Assessments - Kyllonen - 2024 - ETS Research Report Series - Wiley Online Library (https://doi.org/10.1002/ets2.12388)\nciteturn6search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.9 years ago; Crawled: 6 days ago; These focus on personalizing assessment; designing principles for the creation of innovative, interactive digital assessments; developing standards for responsible and ethical AI applications including automated content generation and scoring; and impacting policy and practice through conceptualizing next-generation educational systems that close disparities. ... * ^{4} Testing fairness issues are also considered in International Test Commission (2001, 2013, 2017), International Test Commission and Association of Test Publishers (2022), and Society for Industrial and Organizational Psychology (2018). ... The 2024 European Union AI Act (https://data.consilium.europa.eu/doc/document/ST-5662-2024-INIT/en/pdf) prohibits the “use of AI systems to infer emotions of a natural person in the areas of workplace and education institutions” (p. 108), with safety and medical exceptions, and cautions that “AI systems providing social scoring of natural persons by public or private actors may lead to discriminatory outcomes and the exclusion of certain groups” (p.\n\n# Charting the Future of Assessments\n\nPatrick Kyllonen, \n\nCorresponding Author\n\nPatrick Kyllonen\n\n  * pkyllonen@ets.org\n\nETS Research Institute, ETS, Princeton, New Jersey, United States\n\nCorresponding author: P. Kyllonen, Email: pkyllonen@ets.org\n\nFirst published: 21 November 2024\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1002/ets2.12388Digital Object Identifier (DOI)\n\n## Abstract\n\nAssessment refers to a broad array of approaches for measuring or evaluating a person's (or group of persons') skills, behaviors, dispositions, or other attributes. Assessments range from standardized tests used in admissions, employee selection, licensure examinations, and domestic and international large-scale assessments of cognitive and behavioral skills to formative K–12 classroom curricular assessments. The various types of assessments are used for a wide variety of purposes, but they also have many common elements, such as standards for their reliability, validity, and fairness—even classroom assessments have standards.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWhat Does the 2025 Review Say About Continuous Norming? | Personality Report (https://personalityreport.org/journal/2025-review-continuous-norming-methods)\nciteturn6search9 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 weeks ago; Crawled: last week; The 2025 systematic review describes varied continuous norming practices but does not establish one method as generally superior: its direct comparisons covered eight studies in seven publications and were inconclusive. ... Cross-validation can test performance on withheld observations from the study’s data-generating setting; it does not automatically establish transport to a new test, population, predictor range, or decision. ... Sources: Continuous Norming Approaches: A Systematic Review and Real Data Example; Standards for Educational and Psychological Testing (2014 edition)\nNor does a norm establish score precision: reliability and measurement error concern how consistently or precisely a score is estimated, whereas a percentile tells where the score sits relative to the reference distribution. A report may state a clear percentile and still leave a reader unable to judge whether small differences are meaningful or whether the scale represents the intended tendency.\n\nThe *Standards for Educational and Psychological Testing (2014 edition)* frames validity around evidence supporting particular interpretations of scores for particular uses. That makes the next step a question about the inference being made: what does this score mean here, and what decision or action is it intended to inform? Evidence that supports a self-reflection prompt does not automatically justify coaching conclusions, and evidence for a coaching interpretation does not on its own establish suitability for employment decisions. A norm supplies the comparison component; validity evidence must support the interpretation and proposed use, with a clear rationale for how the score contributes. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFull article: Towards a code of ethics for using technology-enabled data and related analytic approaches in educational assessment (https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/0969594X.2025.2453138)\nciteturn6search10 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.8 years ago; Crawled: last month; A recent systematic review showed that surprisingly few studies considered ethical, privacy and regulatory issues associated with the use of TEDRAA in educational assessment (Murchan & Siddiq, Citation2021). ... As highlighted in the introduction, test standards such as the Standards for Educational and Psychological Testing (AERA, APA, NCME, Citation2014) provide guidance to test developers and other stakeholders in relation to the appropriate design, administration, scoring, interpretation and use of tests. ... They range in date from 1995 to 2014 and range widely in focus, some emphasising different phases of test development and some focusing on broader concepts and practices in assessment. ... A process of discussion and consensus-building yielded a number of areas within each standard that could reasonably be linked with TEDRAA, as presented in [Button: Table 4].\n\nFirst, we identified six widely used professional standards for educational assessment (see [Button: Table 4]). As highlighted in the introduction, test standards such as the Standards for Educational and Psychological Testing (AERA, APA, NCME, Citation2014) provide guidance to test developers and other stakeholders in relation to the appropriate design, administration, scoring, interpretation and use of tests. The 2014 Standards, which were revised a number of times since 1966, are probably the most visible and enduring guidelines influencing assessment internationally. Although, the Standards were developed in the US context, they have achieved widespread appeal in establishing expected norms of good practice in assessment and providing a basis for evaluating the quality of those practices. The standards are broad-based, and although they do ‘not contain enforcement mechanisms’ (p. ix) or constitute ‘a statement of legal requirements’ (p. 1), regulatory authorities and the judicial system in the US frequently refer to the Standards when considering the work of test developers and impact on users. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe GRoNC: Guidelines for Reporting on Norm-Referenced and Criterion-Referenced Scores (https://ouci.dntb.gov.ua/en/works/4wnXkXxd/)\nciteturn6search11 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 days ago; 2025, Assessment, № 6, p. ... Psychological test manuals vary widely in their reporting of the construction and interpretation of standardized scores. ... Because a specific standard for reporting on standardized scores is lacking, we developed Guidelines for Reporting on Norm-referenced and Criterion-referenced Scores (GRoNC), following a systematic approach for creating reporting guidelines (EQUATOR). The development took place in two stages: Stage 1, developing a preliminary version of the GRoNC based on a literature review; Stage 2, a Delphi process in two rounds, involving both theoretical experts ( <jats:italic toggle=\"yes\">n = 11) and test developers ( <jats:italic toggle=\"yes\">n = 14).\n\n# The GRoNC: Guidelines for Reporting on Norm-Referenced and Criterion-Referenced Scores\n\n## General information\n\nPublication type\n\nJournal Article\n\nDOI\n\n10.1177/10731911251371395\n\nJournal\n\n2025, Assessment, № 6, p. 954-972\n\nPublisher\n\nSAGE Publications\n\nAuthors\n\nMarieke E. Timmerman, Annelies De Bildt, Julian Urban\n\n## Details\n\nNumber of citations\n\n4\n\nNumber of works in the list of references\n\n77\n\nIndexed in Scopus\n\nYes\n\nIndexed in Web of Science\n\nYes\n\n## Abstract\n\nPsychological test manuals vary widely in their reporting of the construction and interpretation of standardized scores. Consequently, the critical evaluation of norm quality and meaning is difficult for test users and reviewers. Because a specific standard for reporting on standardized scores is lacking, we developed Guidelines for Reporting on Norm-referenced and Criterion-referenced Scores (GRoNC), following a systematic approach for creating reporting guidelines (EQUATOR). The development took place in two stages: Stage 1, developing a preliminary version of the GRoNC based on a literature review; Stage 2, a Delphi process in two rounds, involving both theoretical experts ( <jats:italic toggle=\"yes\">n = 11) and test developers ( <jats:italic toggle=\"yes\">n = 14). The GRoNC includes a series of questions and associated explanations. It supports test developers in developing and reporting upon their standardized scores, and reviewers in evaluating a psychological test on its standardized scores. We provide recommendations on using the GRoNC and conclude by describing our expectations and plans to increase the impact of the GRoNC on reporting practice.\n"
